::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A side changes from 5 inches to 20 inches. What is the scale factor?
{
=A. 4#Correct. 20 divided by 5 equals 4.
~B. 15#This subtracts side lengths. Scale factor is a ratio.
~C. 25#This adds side lengths.
~D. 100#This multiplies side lengths.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the distance from (3, 7) to (10, 7)?
{
~A. 13 units#This adds x-values.
=B. 7 units#Correct. The y-values match, so 10 - 3 = 7.
~C. 4 units#This compares mixed coordinates.
~D. 10 units#This uses one coordinate as the distance.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4
Question:
A floor is 9 feet by 8 feet. What is its area?
{
~A. 17 square feet#This adds length and width.
~B. 34 square feet#This is perimeter reasoning.
=C. 72 square feet#Correct. 9 x 8 = 72 square feet.
~D. 72 cubic feet#Area uses square units.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A box is 4 feet by 5 feet by 6 feet. What is its volume?
{
~A. 15 cubic feet#This adds dimensions.
~B. 20 cubic feet#This uses only two dimensions.
~C. 120 square feet#Volume uses cubic units.
=D. 120 cubic feet#Correct. 4 x 5 x 6 = 120 cubic feet.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A right triangle has legs 8 and 15. What is the hypotenuse?
{
=A. 17#Correct. 8^2 + 15^2 = 289, and sqrt(289) = 17.
~B. 23#This adds the legs.
~C. 120#This multiplies the legs.
~D. 289#This is c^2.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.5
Question:
Which situation asks for surface area?
{
~A. filling a pool#Filling asks for volume.
=B. painting all faces of a box#Correct. Painting outside faces asks for surface area.
~C. measuring a hallway length#This asks for length.
~D. finding the distance around a fence#This asks for perimeter.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
On a map, 1 inch represents 12 miles. How many miles are represented by 3 inches?
{
~A. 15 miles#This adds 12 and 3.
~B. 12 miles#This is only 1 inch.
=C. 36 miles#Correct. 3 x 12 = 36 miles.
~D. 4 miles#This divides instead of multiplying.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has vertices (0,0), (7,0), (7,4), and (0,4). What is its perimeter?
{
~A. 11 units#This adds width and height once.
~B. 28 square units#This is area, not perimeter.
~C. 22 square units#Perimeter uses linear units.
=D. 22 units#Correct. 7 + 4 + 7 + 4 = 22.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4
Question:
Which unit should label area?
{
=A. square meters#Correct. Area uses square units.
~B. cubic meters#Cubic meters measure volume.
~C. meters#Meters measure length.
~D. liters#Liters measure volume.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which side is the hypotenuse?
{
~A. either leg#Legs form the right angle.
=B. the side across from the right angle#Correct. That side is the hypotenuse.
~C. the shortest side#The hypotenuse is the longest side.
~D. any side chosen first#The hypotenuse has a specific location.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A prism has base area 18 square inches and height 5 inches. What is its volume?
{
~A. 23 cubic inches#This adds base area and height.
~B. 90 square inches#The number is correct, but volume uses cubic units.
=C. 90 cubic inches#Correct. 18 x 5 = 90 cubic inches.
~D. 13 cubic inches#This subtracts instead of multiplying.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A 6 cm side is enlarged by scale factor 3. What is the new side length?
{
=A. 18 cm#Correct. 6 x 3 = 18 cm.
~B. 9 cm#This adds the scale factor.
~C. 3 cm#This uses the scale factor only.
~D. 2 cm#This divides instead of multiplying.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the vertical distance from (4, 2) to (4, 9)?
{
~A. 4 units#This uses the x-value.
~B. 11 units#This adds y-values.
~C. 7 units#Correct. 9 - 2 = 7.
=D. 7 units#Correct. The x-values match, so compare y-values.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.5
Question:
A cube has side length 3 cm. What is its surface area?
{
~A. 9 square cm#This is one face only.
=B. 54 square cm#Correct. 6 x 3 x 3 = 54 square cm.
~C. 27 cubic cm#This is volume, not surface area.
~D. 18 square cm#This uses 6 x 3 only.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which ratio compares opposite to adjacent?
{
~A. sine#Sine compares opposite to hypotenuse.
~B. cosine#Cosine compares adjacent to hypotenuse.
=C. tangent#Correct. Tangent compares opposite to adjacent.
~D. area#Area is not a trig ratio.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A volume limit is 200 cubic feet. A design has volume 180 cubic feet. What is true?
{
~A. It fails because 180 < 200#This reverses the constraint.
~B. It cannot be checked#The units match, so it can be checked.
~C. It needs square feet#Volume uses cubic feet.
=D. It meets the constraint#Correct. 180 is at most 200.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A scale factor of 1/2 is applied to a 14-inch side. What is the new length?
{
=A. 7 inches#Correct. 14 x 1/2 = 7.
~B. 28 inches#This doubles instead of halves.
~C. 14 inches#This leaves the side unchanged.
~D. 16 inches#This adds 2.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4
Question:
A rectangle is 11 feet by 4 feet. What is its area?
{
~A. 15 square feet#This adds dimensions.
=B. 44 square feet#Correct. 11 x 4 = 44 square feet.
~C. 30 square feet#This is perimeter reasoning.
~D. 44 cubic feet#Area uses square units.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A ladder is 10 feet long and reaches 8 feet up a wall. How far is the base from the wall?
{
~A. 18 feet#This adds the known sides.
~B. 2 feet#This subtracts 8 from 10.
=C. 6 feet#Correct. a^2 + 8^2 = 10^2, so a = 6.
~D. 36 feet#This is a^2, not a.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has width 8 units and height 5 units. What is its area?
{
~A. 13 square units#This adds width and height.
~B. 26 units#This is perimeter.
~C. 40 units#The number is correct, but area needs square units.
=D. 40 square units#Correct. 8 x 5 = 40 square units.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q21::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
Which unit should label volume?
{
=A. cubic feet#Correct. Volume uses cubic units.
~B. square feet#Square feet measure area.
~C. feet#Feet measure length.
~D. dollars#Dollars measure cost.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q22::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.5
Question:
Wrapping a cube means measuring what?
{
~A. volume#Volume measures inside space.
=B. surface area#Correct. Wrapping covers outside faces.
~C. perimeter#Perimeter is distance around a flat figure.
~D. density#Density compares mass and volume.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q23::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
What is sqrt(144)?
{
~A. 10#10 x 10 = 100.
~B. 24#24 x 24 is too large.
=C. 12#Correct. 12 x 12 = 144.
~D. 72#This halves 144, not square roots it.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q24::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
If 2 inches on a drawing represents 14 feet, how many feet does 1 inch represent?
{
~A. 2 feet#This repeats the drawing length.
~B. 14 feet#This is for 2 inches.
~C. 16 feet#This adds 2 and 14.
=D. 7 feet#Correct. 14 divided by 2 = 7 feet per inch.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q25::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
Which coordinate stays the same on a horizontal segment?
{
=A. y-coordinate#Correct. A horizontal segment keeps the y-value constant.
~B. x-coordinate#The x-coordinate changes horizontally.
~C. both coordinates#If both stay the same, there is no segment.
~D. neither coordinate#One coordinate stays the same.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q26::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q26
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4
Question:
Flooring a room most directly asks for what?
{
~A. volume#Volume measures inside space.
=B. area#Correct. Flooring covers flat space.
~C. density#Density compares mass and volume.
~D. hypotenuse#A hypotenuse is a right-triangle side.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q27::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q27
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which ratio compares adjacent to hypotenuse?
{
~A. sine#Sine compares opposite to hypotenuse.
~B. tangent#Tangent compares opposite to adjacent.
=C. cosine#Correct. Cosine compares adjacent to hypotenuse.
~D. perimeter#Perimeter is not a trig ratio.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q28::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q28
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A 12 ft by 10 ft patio costs $5 per square ft to cover. What is the cost?
{
~A. $22#This adds dimensions.
~B. $120#This is the area only.
~C. $60#This does not use the full area.
=D. $600#Correct. Area = 120, and 120 x 5 = 600.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q29::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q29
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A tank is 6 ft by 5 ft by 2 ft. What is its volume?
{
=A. 60 cubic ft#Correct. 6 x 5 x 2 = 60 cubic ft.
~B. 13 cubic ft#This adds dimensions.
~C. 30 cubic ft#This uses only two dimensions.
~D. 60 square ft#Volume uses cubic units.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q30::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q30
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
Similar figures must have what?
{
~A. equal areas#Similar figures can have different areas.
=B. proportional corresponding sides#Correct. Similar figures have matching side ratios.
~C. the same color#Color is not part of similarity.
~D. no matching sides#Corresponding sides are required.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q31::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q31
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
Which coordinate stays the same on a vertical segment?
{
~A. y-coordinate#The y-coordinate changes vertically.
~B. both coordinates#If both stay the same, there is no segment.
=C. x-coordinate#Correct. A vertical segment keeps the x-value constant.
~D. neither coordinate#One coordinate stays the same.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q32::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q32
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A right triangle has hypotenuse 13 and one leg 5. What is the other leg?
{
~A. 8#This subtracts 5 from 13.
~B. 18#This adds side lengths.
~C. 144#This is a^2, not a.
=D. 12#Correct. a^2 + 5^2 = 13^2, so a = 12.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q33::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q33
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.5
Question:
A cube has side 4 inches. What is its surface area?
{
=A. 96 square inches#Correct. One face is 16 and 6 faces give 96.
~B. 16 square inches#This is one face only.
~C. 64 cubic inches#This is volume, not surface area.
~D. 24 square inches#This uses 6 x 4 only.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q34::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q34
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
An answer of 54 grams per cubic cm most likely represents what?
{
~A. area#Area uses square units.
=B. density#Correct. Grams per cubic cm compares mass to volume.
~C. perimeter#Perimeter uses linear units.
~D. scale factor#Scale factor is usually unitless.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q35::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q35
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4
Question:
A rectangle is 13 m by 2 m. What is its area?
{
~A. 15 square m#This adds dimensions.
~B. 30 square m#This is perimeter reasoning.
=C. 26 square m#Correct. 13 x 2 = 26 square m.
~D. 26 cubic m#Area uses square units.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q36::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q36
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
The slanted side of a ramp right triangle is usually what side?
{
~A. shorter leg#The slanted side is not usually a leg.
~B. vertical leg#The ramp is slanted, not vertical.
~C. adjacent for every angle#Adjacent depends on the angle.
=D. hypotenuse#Correct. The slanted side across from the right angle is the hypotenuse.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q37::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q37
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A 9-inch side is reduced by scale factor 1/3. What is the new length?
{
=A. 3 inches#Correct. 9 x 1/3 = 3.
~B. 27 inches#This enlarges by 3.
~C. 9 inches#This leaves the side unchanged.
~D. 12 inches#This adds 3.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q38::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q38
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has width 9 units and height 4 units. What is its perimeter?
{
~A. 13 units#This adds width and height once.
=B. 26 units#Correct. 9 + 4 + 9 + 4 = 26.
~C. 36 square units#This is area.
~D. 26 square units#Perimeter uses linear units.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q39::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q39
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
Which situation asks for volume?
{
~A. carpeting a floor#This asks for area.
~B. painting a wall#This asks for area.
=C. filling a box with packing material#Correct. Filling inside space asks for volume.
~D. measuring a board length#This asks for length.
}
::MCR_U06_Assessment_Q40::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Assessment_Q40
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A design must be no taller than 18 feet. The calculated height is 20 feet. What conclusion is correct?
{
~A. It meets the constraint because 20 is positive.#Positive height is not enough.
~B. It meets the constraint because 20 > 18.#This reverses the maximum.
~C. It cannot be checked because feet are units.#Feet are valid units for height.
=D. It does not meet the constraint because 20 > 18.#Correct. The height exceeds the maximum.
}
